# 01 Entities and fields
#
# Shows how to declare a table (an entity) and the closed set of field types.
#   - every field is required, defaulted (`= v`) or optional (`?`)
#   - types: text, int, money, bool, date, datetime, duration(unit), id
#   - `text(64)` bounds a text field or parameter to 64 characters. The
#     database keeps the bound on an open column and the write path keeps
#     it on a sealed one. An over-long parameter answers `bound_violation`
#     at the door; an over-long field value answers `check_violation`.
#   - `(key: prod)` picks the prefix of the typed id (prod_01J8...)
#   - `id` and `rev` exist on every entity and are never declared
#
# Reference: docs/definition-language.md section 3 "Syntax", section 2 "Types".

domain shop @ 1.0.0

entity products (key: prod) {
  name:     text(120)                # required, at most 120 characters
  price:    money                    # exact decimal, minor units on disk
  stock:    int = 0                  # defaulted
  active:   bool = true
  sku:      text?                    # optional: null until set
  launched: date?
  updated:  datetime?
  restock:  duration(d) = 14d        # a duration always names its unit
  buyer:    id?                      # an id of any entity
}

# A read: source, projection, then steps. `id` and `rev` are implicit fields.
op listProducts unbounded =
  products[id, rev, name, price, stock, active]
  sort name

# A bounded text parameter: the door refuses a longer value before planning.
op renameProduct($product: id, $name: text(120)) =
  set products(id == $product) { name: $name }
